.TH DMESG 1
.SH NAME
dmesg \- the log since boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmesg
.RB [ \-s
.I file
|
.BR \-w ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dmesg
prints the console's log, what a serial wire shows: the kernel's messages
and every server's, since boot (the last 32 KiB:
.BR sys_log (2)).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Save the log into
.I file
instead.
.TP
.B \-w
(init starts it at boot) Save the log to
.I /etc/boot.log
20 s, 1, 2 and 5 minutes after starting, then end: on a new machine, the
disk read on another computer tells what was found. Only up to the
console's first login prompt (what a session on the console prints after
it is the user's); the file is root's only (mode 0600).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0; 1 if the file cannot be written; 2 for a usage error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/boot.log
The log of the last boot, up to the first login prompt.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_log (2),
.BR con (4),
.BR init (8)
